WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.280 --> 00:00:05.510
"Getting one hundred ninety-five countries to agree costs billions and takes forever.".

2
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:09.820
No. Getting countries to *implement* things costs billions.

3
00:00:10.580 --> 00:00:15.120
Getting them to *sign papers* is surprisingly cheap. Humans love signing papers.

4
00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:22.540
It makes them feel important. What Treaties Actually Cost. N.G.O.

5
00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:28.560
campaign budgets are rarely disclosed publicly, because nonprofits guard their finances the way dragons guard gold.

6
00:00:29.160 --> 00:00:34.920
But we know enough to establish something surprising: treaty campaigns run on pocket change.

7
00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:43.800
The Ottawa Treaty banning landmines took fourteen months. It started with one staff member, Jody Williams, who grew a coalition

8
00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:51.340
to over a thousand organizations across sixty countries. Canada committed one hundred million Canadian dollars over ten

9
00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:58.100
years; international donors added one hundred sixty-nine million dollars in nineteen ninety-seven for mine action.

10
00:00:58.580 --> 00:01:02.960
One hundred twenty-two states signed. She won the Nobel Peace Prize.

11
00:01:04.379 --> 00:01:11.180
The Cluster Munitions Convention took about two years. A coalition of N.G.O.s backed by a core group of seven governments

12
00:01:11.180 --> 00:01:17.680
got ninety-four states to sign and over a million stockpiled munitions destroyed.

13
00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:23.160
The Arms Trade Treaty took a decade but started with just three supporting governments.

14
00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:29.440
The Control Arms coalition, led by Oxfam and Amnesty International, published more than fifty reports and built support

15
00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:35.940
until one hundred thirty countries signed. The Nuclear Ban Treaty also took a decade of campaigning.

16
00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:44.460
ICAN ran the entire operation with five staff in a Geneva office, coordinating four hundred sixty-eight partner organizations.

17
00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:49.940
One hundred twenty-two states voted yes. Another Nobel Peace Prize.

18
00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:56.360
Pattern: One woman with a phone convinced one hundred twenty-two countries to ban landmines.

19
00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:01.300
Five people in a Geneva office won a Nobel Prize. The N.G.O.

20
00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:06.860
costs are a few million dollars per year. Government conference costs add ten to thirty million dollars.

21
00:02:07.840 --> 00:02:12.220
Total to get a treaty signed: fifteen to fifty million dollars.

22
00:02:12.340 --> 00:02:15.720
Your species spends more than that on Super Bowl ads for beer.

23
00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:21.740
Inflation-Adjusted to twenty-twenty-four Dollars. Using B.L.S.

24
00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:28.680
C.P.I. data: The Ottawa Treaty in nineteen ninety-seven had an original cost estimate of fifteen to twenty-five million

25
00:02:28.680 --> 00:02:36.200
dollars, which is thirty to fifty million in twenty-twenty-four dollars, an inflation multiplier of about two times.

26
00:02:36.440 --> 00:02:43.180
The Cluster Munitions Convention in two thousand eight had an original cost estimate of fifteen to twenty-five million dollars,

27
00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:50.060
which is twenty-three to thirty-eight million in twenty-twenty-four dollars, an inflation multiplier of about one point

28
00:02:50.060 --> 00:02:58.020
five. The Arms Trade Treaty in twenty-thirteen had an original cost estimate of twenty to thirty million dollars, which

29
00:02:58.020 --> 00:03:04.580
is twenty-seven to forty-one million in twenty-twenty-four dollars, an inflation multiplier of about one point three five.

30
00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:13.120
The Nuclear Ban Treaty in twenty-seventeen had an original cost estimate of ten to twenty million dollars, which is thirteen

31
00:03:13.120 --> 00:03:18.900
to twenty-five million in twenty-twenty-four dollars, an inflation multiplier of about one point two five.

32
00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:26.780
In today's money: Treaty campaigns cost roughly twenty-five to fifty million dollars (twenty-twenty-four U.S.D.).

33
00:03:27.440 --> 00:03:30.960
That's less than zero point two percent of the implementation cost.

34
00:03:31.840 --> 00:03:38.720
Banning landmines: about forty million dollars to get the signatures, and more than five billion dollars to actually dig

35
00:03:38.720 --> 00:03:45.040
them up. The paperwork is the cheap part. The not-exploding-anymore part is where it gets expensive.

36
00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:54.620
Cost for a one percent Health Treaty. This one touches military budgets, the sacred cow of national sovereignty.

37
00:03:55.400 --> 00:04:01.480
Suggest moving one percent of the explosion money and politicians look at you like you've asked to borrow their favorite

38
00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:09.960
child. So budget three to four times what landmines cost. Phase one: Pre-negotiations (Coalition building, expert drafts)

39
00:04:10.060 --> 00:04:17.459
lasts eighteen to twenty-four months and costs eight to twelve million dollars, funded by philanthropy (Gates, Open Philanthropy,

40
00:04:17.519 --> 00:04:25.700
et cetera) and champion governments. Phase two: Global Campaign (Platform, three point five percent consent, media) lasts

41
00:04:25.700 --> 00:04:31.600
three to five years and costs forty-five to eighty million dollars, funded by philanthropy and crowd-funding.

42
00:04:32.720 --> 00:04:41.140
Phase three: Diplomatic Process (Conferences, delegate travel) lasts eighteen to thirty months and costs fifteen to twenty-five

43
00:04:41.140 --> 00:04:45.120
million dollars, funded by host governments and the U.N. Trust Fund.

44
00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:54.440
Phase four: Ratification Drive (Legal reviews, parliamentary aid) lasts two to four years and costs five to eight million

45
00:04:54.440 --> 00:05:03.600
dollars, funded by philanthropy. Phase five: Contingency (Opposition management) is ongoing and costs ten to fifteen million

46
00:05:03.600 --> 00:05:10.820
dollars for strategic communications and lobbying. Total: eighty-three to one hundred forty million dollars.

47
00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:17.060
The Math. Signing the treaty: approximately eighty-three million dollars.

48
00:05:17.720 --> 00:05:22.760
What the treaty unlocks: twenty-seven point two billion dollars per year.

49
00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:27.400
Forever. That's a three hundred to one return on paperwork.

50
00:05:28.480 --> 00:05:36.440
How to Make Governments Sign Things. Politicians move when they see (a) voters demanding it and (b) political cover.

51
00:05:37.320 --> 00:05:45.680
Give them both: Prove public demand: Get three point five percent of the population (two hundred eighty million people)

52
00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:53.760
to say "yes" with verified identity. Harvard researcher Erica Chenoweth found that every nonviolent campaign with three

53
00:05:53.760 --> 00:05:58.140
point five percent plus participation succeeded. That's not activism.

54
00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:06.120
That's a mandate. Two hundred eighty million verified humans is the kind of number that makes politicians suddenly remember

55
00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:15.180
they work for you. Prove it's safe: Peer-reviewed modeling showing every nation gains more in health savings than it loses

56
00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:19.820
in military spending. Politicians need cover the way vampires need darkness.

57
00:06:20.700 --> 00:06:25.820
Give them studies to wave around. Timeline to "Governments Can't Ignore This".

58
00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:34.440
With approximately sixty million dollars in seed funding, you can reach "Treaty-Ready" status in two to three years: Platform:

59
00:06:34.780 --> 00:06:38.960
two hundred eighty million verified supporters (three point five percent threshold).

60
00:06:39.720 --> 00:06:48.120
Modeling: Country-by-country impact analysis. Coalition: one hundred twenty plus supportive N.G.O.s and five to ten champion

61
00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:53.200
governments. Once these assets exist, formal negotiations become inevitable.

62
00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:59.940
Not because politicians want them. Because ignoring two hundred eighty million people is the kind of career decision that

63
00:06:59.940 --> 00:07:04.700
leads to "spending more time with family.". You aren't buying a treaty.

64
00:07:05.160 --> 00:07:07.900
You're buying the machinery that makes the treaty inevitable.

65
00:07:09.460 --> 00:07:16.920
Political Success Probability: Economic Analysis. To calculate expected value, you need to estimate the probability this

66
00:07:16.920 --> 00:07:24.020
actually works. This section documents the rationale for the one percent central estimate with a zero point one percent

67
00:07:24.220 --> 00:07:30.520
to twenty-five percent confidence interval. Yes, the model assumes a ninety-nine percent chance of failure.

68
00:07:31.240 --> 00:07:39.220
It's still a good bet. Stay with me. Even at a one percent probability of success, this intervention's expected cost per

69
00:07:39.220 --> 00:07:48.020
disability-adjusted life year, or D.A.L.Y., of seventeen point seven cents is still five hundred three times more cost-effective

70
00:07:48.020 --> 00:07:55.120
than malaria bed nets at eighty-nine dollars per D.A.L.Y. Bed nets are the gold standard of global health interventions.

71
00:07:56.260 --> 00:08:05.240
Historical Precedents (The Depressing Table). The zero point seven percent Official Development Assistance, or O.D.A.

72
00:08:05.500 --> 00:08:14.420
target, aimed for zero point seven percent of Gross National Income, or G.N.I., to foreign aid.

73
00:08:14.420 --> 00:08:19.500
About twenty percent of countries actually did it over more than fifty years.

74
00:08:20.560 --> 00:08:27.680
Scandinavia and Germany. That's basically it. The Kyoto Protocol set binding emissions targets.

75
00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:36.059
About fifty-five percent of countries complied initially, then it collapsed between nineteen ninety-seven and twenty twelve.

76
00:08:36.700 --> 00:08:45.460
The U.S. never ratified. Canada withdrew. Classic. The Paris Agreement involves non-binding pledges.

77
00:08:45.460 --> 00:08:49.670
About fifteen to twenty-five percent are on track since twenty fifteen.

78
00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:54.800
One hundred ninety-five countries signed. Maybe forty meant it.

79
00:08:55.960 --> 00:09:01.640
The NATO two percent G.D.P. defense target has been in place since twenty fourteen.

80
00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:08.040
About thirty-two percent of members have met it. Even the threat of being invaded wasn't enough.

81
00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:14.900
The E.U. Stability Pact requires a three percent deficit and sixty percent debt limit.

82
00:09:15.660 --> 00:09:18.680
It has been routinely violated since nineteen ninety-seven.

83
00:09:19.420 --> 00:09:28.300
France and Germany broke their own rule. Pattern: International financial commitments achieve less than twenty-five percent

84
00:09:28.300 --> 00:09:34.540
meaningful compliance. Your species signs things and then doesn't do them.

85
00:09:35.020 --> 00:09:42.900
It's like a New Year's resolution, but for geopolitics. Why a one percent Treaty is Harder Than Most Precedents.

86
00:09:43.900 --> 00:09:50.100
Let's be honest about the obstacles. This is not a chapter written by someone trying to sell you something.

87
00:09:50.800 --> 00:09:58.460
(It is. But at least it's honest about the hard parts.). It touches military budgets, and politicians would rather discuss

88
00:09:58.460 --> 00:10:07.320
their browser history than their defense budget. It requires ongoing annual allocation instead of a one-time signature,

89
00:10:07.400 --> 00:10:12.020
functioning like a gym membership for peace that is paid monthly, forever.

90
00:10:12.940 --> 00:10:20.580
There is no immediate security threat to motivate governments without an active catastrophe, even though one hundred fifty

91
00:10:20.580 --> 00:10:25.180
thousand daily disease deaths apparently doesn't qualify as one.

92
00:10:26.200 --> 00:10:34.900
The novel mechanism of military-to-health reallocation has never been done, and humans are suspicious of things they haven't

93
00:10:34.980 --> 00:10:40.200
tried before (which is odd for a species that also invented bungee jumping).

94
00:10:40.820 --> 00:10:49.060
A coordination problem exists because benefits require critical mass, meaning nobody wants to go first and everybody wants

95
00:10:49.060 --> 00:10:53.720
to go second. Why a one percent Treaty Might Beat the Odds.

96
00:10:54.740 --> 00:11:02.480
It is self-funding and creates health dividends that exceed the "cost," meaning you make money doing this, which is unusual

97
00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:09.280
for moral obligations. It has bipartisan appeal because "Should we cure more diseases?"

98
00:11:09.680 --> 00:11:14.160
polls well across every political spectrum, which is shocking, I know.

99
00:11:14.920 --> 00:11:22.620
A referendum pathway can bypass government resistance through direct democracy, which is the one weird trick politicians

100
00:11:22.620 --> 00:11:30.780
hate. There are visible beneficiaries from the one hundred fifty thousand daily disease deaths, making it hard to argue

101
00:11:30.780 --> 00:11:39.160
with the body count. Network effects mean each signing country makes the next more likely, as peer pressure is humanity's

102
00:11:39.160 --> 00:11:48.820
most reliable technology. Probability Estimates. Economists would find the following defensible (and economists find very

103
00:11:48.820 --> 00:11:55.000
little defensible): For the pessimistic floor scenario, the probability is zero point one percent.

104
00:11:56.020 --> 00:12:01.040
Everything goes wrong: global crises, political gridlock, and possibly an asteroid.

105
00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:05.460
For the conservative central scenario, the probability is one percent.

106
00:12:06.280 --> 00:12:11.920
There is a ninety-nine percent chance of failure, but it is still five hundred three times better than bed nets.

107
00:12:13.100 --> 00:12:17.520
For the optimistic ceiling scenario, the probability is twenty-five percent.

108
00:12:18.460 --> 00:12:23.780
A major crisis opens a political window, or humans just decide to be smart for once.

109
00:12:24.760 --> 00:12:30.920
The model uses: Central estimate one percent. Expected Value (Even When You Assume Failure).

110
00:12:31.860 --> 00:12:39.800
At one percent probability of success: The expected R.O.I. is eight hundred forty-eight thousand to one (still positive,

111
00:12:39.880 --> 00:12:48.440
even assuming near-certain failure). The expected cost per disability-adjusted life year, or D.A.L.Y., is zero point one

112
00:12:48.440 --> 00:12:55.500
seven seven dollars (five hundred three times better than bed nets at eighty-nine dollars per D.A.L.Y.).

113
00:12:55.560 --> 00:13:00.940
The downside is capped at about one billion dollars, and you lose the cost of a nice yacht.

114
00:13:01.640 --> 00:13:10.940
The upside is unbounded, and you cure most diseases. Even assuming a ninety-nine percent chance of total failure, this beats

115
00:13:10.940 --> 00:13:16.420
the gold standard of global health interventions. The math doesn't care about your pessimism.

116
00:13:17.660 --> 00:13:24.340
Why The Treaty Won't Stop at one percent Most international commitments stagnate after signing.

117
00:13:24.940 --> 00:13:32.700
You saw the depressing table. Zero point seven percent Official Development Assistance, or O.D.A., targets have been unmet

118
00:13:32.700 --> 00:13:41.140
for over fifty years. Kyoto abandoned. Paris missed. Your track record with follow-through makes goldfish look disciplined.

119
00:13:41.940 --> 00:13:48.980
So why would a one percent health treaty be any different? Because this time, people get paid.

120
00:13:50.020 --> 00:13:57.180
The eighty ten ten funding structure allocates ten percent of treaty funding (two point seven two billion dollars per year

121
00:13:57.180 --> 00:14:04.880
at one percent) to Incentive Alignment Bonds, or I.A.B.s. These are securities that pay out when treaties *expand*, not

122
00:14:04.880 --> 00:14:10.300
just maintain. In other words: people get rich when more diseases get cured.

123
00:14:11.040 --> 00:14:14.540
Finally, a financial instrument that isn't morally bankrupt.

124
00:14:15.520 --> 00:14:20.660
The Expansion Mechanism. At the one percent treaty level, the annual I.A.B.

125
00:14:21.020 --> 00:14:28.420
pool is two point seven two billion dollars per year. Political pressure is strong, which is ten times the size of the largest

126
00:14:28.420 --> 00:14:33.500
N.G.O. budgets. At the two percent treaty level, the annual I.A.B.

127
00:14:33.720 --> 00:14:40.880
pool is five point four billion dollars per year. Political pressure is very strong, reaching the nation-state level.

128
00:14:41.720 --> 00:14:48.400
At the five percent treaty level, the annual I.A.B. pool is thirteen point five billion dollars per year.

129
00:14:49.020 --> 00:14:53.140
Political pressure is overwhelming, equivalent to a major industry lobby.

130
00:14:54.240 --> 00:15:00.780
At the ten percent treaty level, the annual I.A.B. pool is twenty-seven billion dollars per year.

131
00:15:01.340 --> 00:15:05.880
Political pressure is unstoppable, as there is no historical precedent.

132
00:15:07.280 --> 00:15:13.280
Why This Differs from Every Other Treaty. Zero point seven percent O.D.A.

133
00:15:13.280 --> 00:15:20.020
failed because nobody made money when countries complied. The N.G.O.s pushing for compliance have small budgets, compete

134
00:15:20.020 --> 00:15:25.130
with each other, and run on guilt. Guilt has terrible fuel efficiency.

135
00:15:25.880 --> 00:15:29.650
The one percent health treaty creates its own expansion lobby.

136
00:15:30.120 --> 00:15:35.220
Bond holders profit when the treaty expands to two percent, then five percent, then ten percent.

137
00:15:36.200 --> 00:15:43.420
These aren't activists you can ignore at a dinner party. They're investors with quarterly earnings calls about curing diseases.

138
00:15:44.160 --> 00:15:50.180
The Ratchet Effect. One. Two point seven two billion dollars per year flows to I.A.B.

139
00:15:50.240 --> 00:15:57.440
holders pushing for two percent. Two. When two percent passes, five point four billion dollars per year pushes for five

140
00:15:57.440 --> 00:16:04.280
percent. Three. When five percent passes, thirteen point five billion dollars per year pushes for ten percent.

141
00:16:05.140 --> 00:16:09.500
Four. The political pressure for expansion *accelerates* with each success.

142
00:16:10.540 --> 00:16:15.390
Most treaties are gym memberships purchased in January and abandoned by February.

143
00:16:15.870 --> 00:16:21.200
I.A.B.s are the opposite: the constituency for expansion *grows* with each success.

144
00:16:21.920 --> 00:16:30.520
Greed doesn't take holidays. Long-Term Feasibility. With I.A.B.s, the ten percent allocation creates a perpetual political

145
00:16:30.520 --> 00:16:37.380
engine. A machine that runs on greed and outputs health. Embarrassing, but effective.

146
00:16:38.060 --> 00:16:44.940
Without I.A.B.s, this joins Kyoto in the Museum of Things Humanity Promised to Do and Then Didn't.
